<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Active primary progressive multiple sclerosis (G35.B1)</content>. MRI brain: fourteen T2 lesions, periventricular, juxtacortical and infratentorial (pons and left middle cerebellar peduncle), one enhancing right periventricular lesion. MRI spine: three cord lesions at C3, C5 and T6, each less than two segments long, with no compression. With more than a year of gradually worsening walking and no relapses, these meet the 1971 McDonald criteria for primary progressive multiple sclerosis (brain lesions in characteristic regions and two or more cord lesions), and the enhancing lesion makes it active. B12, TSH and HIV results exclude the main mimics. Dr. Watanabe advises ocrelizumab. Screening before therapy: hepatitis B surface antigen, core antibody and surface antibody all negative (not infected, not immune); immunoglobulins, liver tests and blood count normal; varicella IgG and measles, mumps and rubella IgG positive; tuberculosis blood test, HIV and hepatitis C negative; JC virus antibody index 0.14, negative. [S]<list><item>Vaccines today: PCV20, recombinant zoster dose 1, Tdap and hepatitis B dose 1; second zoster and hepatitis B doses in about five weeks.</item><item>First ocrelizumab half-dose at least two weeks after the last vaccine.</item><item>Reproductive plans discussed privately with Dr. Watanabe and recorded in the confidential part of the chart.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Spastic gait and fall risk (R26.89)</content>. Spasticity limits her walking and stiffens her mornings; no falls. [S]<list><item>Start baclofen 5 mg three times a day, increasing to 10 mg three times a day once tolerated.</item><item>Physical therapy evaluation requested at intake; awaiting a first appointment.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Neurogenic bladder (N31.9)</content>. Urgency with a low post-void residual of 40 mL at intake, so a bladder-relaxing medicine is safe. [S]<list><item>Start mirabegron 25 mg daily rather than an anticholinergic, to avoid effects on concentration; blood pressure at each visit.</item><item>Continue timed voiding and fluids earlier in the day.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>New or worsening symptoms (relapse, pseudo-relapse with heat or infection)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No new symptoms; relapse and pseudo-relapse plan explained with a written card</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Disability: EDSS and timed 25-foot walk</td><td>Every visit, and at least every 6 months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>EDSS 6.0; timed 25-foot walk 11.0 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Brain MRI</td><td>At diagnosis with gadolinium; re-baseline 3 to 6 months after starting therapy; then yearly without routine gadolinium</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Diagnostic MRI with gadolinium: fourteen T2 lesions in characteristic regions; one enhancing lesion</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Spinal cord MRI</td><td>At diagnosis; later only for new spinal symptoms or diagnostic doubt</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Three cord lesions; no compression</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hepatitis B surface antigen, core antibody and surface antibody</td><td>Before the first dose</td><td>Up to date</td><td>All negative at intake: not infected, not immune; vaccination started today</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Quantitative immunoglobulins</td><td>Before the first dose, then before each dose</td><td>Up to date</td><td>IgG 1080, IgM 110, IgA 210 mg/dL at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood count with absolute lymphocytes</td><td>Before the first dose, then before each dose</td><td>Up to date</td><td>White cells 6.4, lymphocytes 1.9 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Liver tests</td><td>Before the first dose, then with symptoms and before each dose</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Normal at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Varicella and measles immunity, tuberculosis blood test, HIV, hepatitis C, JC virus antibody</td><td>Before the first dose</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Varicella and measles immune; tuberculosis blood test, HIV and hepatitis C negative; JC virus antibody index 0.14</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Vaccines before therapy (PCV20, recombinant zoster x2, Tdap, hepatitis B x2)</td><td>Inactivated at least 2 weeks and live at least 4 weeks before the first dose</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PCV20, zoster dose 1, Tdap and hepatitis B dose 1 today; no live vaccine needed</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Spasticity (modified Ashworth) and baclofen tolerance</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Modified Ashworth 2 in both legs; baclofen started</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Bladder: urgency, nocturia, urinary infection, and blood pressure on mirabegron</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Urgency and nocturia twice; post-void residual 40 mL at intake; mirabegron started; blood pressure 122/76</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Falls, walking aids and physical therapy</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Ordered</td><td>No falls; physical therapy evaluation requested at intake, first appointment awaited</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Fatigue and heat sensitivity</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Tires by mid-afternoon; pacing and cooling advice given</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>25-hydroxyvitamin D</td><td>At baseline, 8 to 12 weeks after repletion, then yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>17 ng/mL at intake; vitamin D3 started at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>At intake, then every 6 months and with any change</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 6 at intake, mostly fatigue and sleep items</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Workplace accommodation</td><td>At intake and with any change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Letter describing her functional needs, without the diagnosis, given to her to send as she chooses</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Multiple sclerosis neurology review</td><td>At diagnosis, then every 6 months on therapy</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Watanabe today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Diagnosis confirmed and classified, vaccines completed at least two weeks before therapy, and a disease-modifying therapy started within ten weeks. In progress; target 1980-06-29 [S]</item><item>No falls through eight months. In progress; target 1980-10-26 [S]</item><item>25-hydroxyvitamin D at least 30 ng/mL within four months. In progress; target 1980-06-29 [S]</item><item>Up no more than once a night to pass urine within three months. In progress; target 1980-06-29 [S]</item><item>Keep her full teaching and speaking schedule with the accommodations she chooses. In progress; target 1980-10-26 [S]</item><item>No new or enlarging T2 lesions on the re-baseline MRI. In progress; target 1980-10-26 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Immunizations given today: Pneumococcal conjugate vaccine PCV20; Recombinant zoster vaccine, dose 1 of 2; Tetanus, diphtheria, and acellular pertussis vaccine (Tdap); Hepatitis B vaccine (HepB-CpG), dose 1 of 2 [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: Practice guideline recommendations summary: Disease-modifying therapies for adults with multiple sclerosis (Rae-Grant A, et al.; PMID 29686116) (2018); Diagnosis of multiple sclerosis: 2017 revisions of the McDonald criteria (Thompson AJ, et al.; PMID 29275977) (2017); 2021 MAGNIMS-CMSC-NAIMS consensus recommendations on the use of MRI in patients with multiple sclerosis (Wattjes MP, et al.; PMID 34139157) (2021); Practice guideline update summary: Vaccine-preventable infections and immunization in multiple sclerosis (Farez MF, et al.; PMID 31462584) (2019); OCREVUS (ocrelizumab) injection, prescribing information (2026); Multiple sclerosis in adults: management (NG220) (2022); Accommodation and Compliance: Multiple Sclerosis (2026); Introduction to the Americans with Disabilities Act (2026); Adult Immunization Schedule by Age (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Review in five weeks for the second vaccine doses and baclofen titration; first ocrelizumab infusion to be booked at least two weeks after that visit. Same-day call for new or worsening symptoms lasting more than 24 hours. Next scheduled visit 1980-04-20: five-week review: second vaccine doses, baclofen titration, physical therapy and readiness for the first infusion. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
